// The string half of tests/xfail/ref_literal_mutable — a separate fixture because it is a separate
// emitter arm. A string rvalue is materialised by `hoistStringTemp` into a scope-freed `__strtmp`, a
// primitive literal by the `__primtmp` arm beside it, and a rule that covered only one of them would have
// left `readFile(path: "x")` legal while `bump(v: 1)` was not — the same defect with two answers.
//
// Covering both is also what forced the stdlib's `ref string` family (`fs`, `log`, `fmt::parse`,
// `net`, `process`) to say `const ref`, which is what those callees always meant.
fn void take(ref string s) { s = "other"; }
fn int32 main() {
    take(s: "literal");
    return 0;
}
